<Base title="Urban Mobility" description="Matthew Rossi's engagement with urban mobility, transit advocacy, and civic technology.">

  <div class="page-header">
    <h1>Urban Mobility</h1>
    <p>Advocacy, civic tech, and self-directed work in transit and urban systems.</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="civic-tech-heading">
    <h2 id="civic-tech-heading">Civic Technology</h2>
    <p>Living in Chicago put me in contact with the civic-tech community through <a href="https://chihacknight.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ChiHackNight</a>, a weekly gathering where technologists work on open-data projects aimed at urban systems. I contributed a small Ruby library integrating with the Chicago Transit Authority's real-time bus API, and explored GTFS feed structures as a way of understanding how transit agencies expose and organize their operational data.</p>
    <p>That hands-on work with transit data helped me appreciate both the technical infrastructure and the data quality challenges that underpin public transportation information systems — and how much policy and institutional decisions shape what data is made available in the first place.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="advocacy-heading">
    <h2 id="advocacy-heading">Advocacy</h2>
    <p>I've been involved in transit and active transportation advocacy through several organizations, including the <a href="https://www.railpassengers.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rail Passengers Association</a>, <a href="https://www.ridersalliance.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Riders Alliance</a> (New York), and <a href="https://www.transalt.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Transportation Alternatives</a>. This work sits alongside sustained reading on urban mobility, transport history, and the policy and governance frameworks that shape how cities move.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="interests-heading">
    <h2 id="interests-heading">Research Interests</h2>
    <p>I'm particularly interested in transit-oriented development as a policy tool for aligning land use, housing, and mobility objectives — and in the institutional and governance conditions that determine whether TOD succeeds in practice. I'm also drawn to questions of climate adaptation in urban transport systems, and how policy frameworks can support resilient, system-wide responses rather than isolated technical fixes.</p>
  </section>

</Base>
